-- Care Chart week-by-week content, Second trimester (weeks 14-26).
-- Content: Move section drafted with real 5/15/30-min tiers (Roop's
-- 'Expanded Move Edition' review pass); Reset expanded by Claude into the
-- 5-mood-mapped format (Heavy day/A little low/Okay/Good/Really good) to
-- match First trimester's already-locked pattern, since the reviewed edition
-- had simplified Reset to one message per week. Every other card (Nourish,
-- Hydration Goal, Care for yourself, Your Corner, Support Moment, Celebrate
-- This Week, For Your Care Team, What You May Notice) is Roop's reviewed
-- content, unchanged.

insert into care_chart_week_content
  (week_number, trimester, theme_title, mantra, priority, journey, what_you_may_notice, move, nourish, hydration_goal, reset, care_for_yourself, your_corner, support_moment, celebrate_this_week, for_your_care_team)
values
  (14, 'second', 'A gentler beginning', 'You do not have to rush into feeling better.', 'Let returning energy arrive at its own pace.', 'The uterus is rising out of the pelvis, and nausea or fatigue may begin to ease. Your baby is practising small facial movements, though you cannot feel them yet.', ARRAY['A little more energy, or no change yet','Less nausea, lingering nausea, or new heartburn','A growing or firmer-feeling abdomen','Stuffy nose, bleeding gums, or mild headaches']::text[], '{"focus": "Ease back into movement without chasing your pre-pregnancy pace.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk slowly for 3 minutes, then spend 2 minutes rolling your shoulders and circling your ankles.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes at a conversational pace. Finish with 5 minutes of gentle shoulder rolls, ankle circles and calf stretching at a wall.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 20 minutes, then complete two easy rounds: 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 30 seconds of supported calf stretching per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Heavy or nauseated: stay near home and choose the 5-minute option. Anxious: use a steady, familiar route. Feeling energised: complete 30 minutes, but finish with energy left.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Build one satisfying plate: a protein food, a whole grain or starchy vegetable, and something colourful. Try dal, rice and a cooked vegetable; add curd if it suits you.', 'Keep water visible. Pair a few sips with every meal, snack and bathroom break.', '{"heavy_day": "If you expected to feel better by now and don''t, that''s a real, valid disappointment — not a failure to \"do\" second trimester right.", "a_little_low": "Relief and worry can coexist. It''s fine to feel both today.", "okay": "Place one hand on your chest and one on your belly. Take five slow breaths and say: \"I can begin this trimester gently.\"", "good": "If energy is genuinely returning, enjoy it without waiting for it to disappear again.", "really_good": "Let this feel exactly as good as it is."}'::jsonb, 'If skin feels more sensitive, simplify: gentle cleanse, moisturiser and broad-spectrum sunscreen. New or strong actives can wait until you have checked them with your clinician.', 'Choose one small thing you missed in the first trimester — a favourite song, a short outing or an unrushed shower — and make room for it.', 'Ask someone close to you to join a short walk or take one routine task off your list.', 'You reached the second trimester. That is a real milestone, even if today feels ordinary.', 'Bring persistent or worsening headaches, ongoing vomiting, or questions about medicines and supplements to your care team.'),
  (15, 'second', 'Make space for comfort', 'Gentle movement can be care, not a test.', 'Reduce strain as your posture and ligaments begin to change.', 'Your uterus is growing and the supporting ligaments are stretching. Your baby is moving often, but those movements may still be too light to notice.', ARRAY['Brief pulling or sharp sensations low on either side, especially with sudden movement','More vaginal discharge without strong odour or irritation','A changing centre of gravity','More appetite, or appetite that still varies']::text[], '{"focus": "Move smoothly to reduce sudden pulling and support your changing posture.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Take five slow breaths, then do 8 wall pelvic tilts and a 2-minute easy walk.", "steady": "Walk for 8 minutes. Add 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 supported side leg lifts per side and a gentle standing side stretch.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 15 minutes, then complete two rounds of 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 chair sit-to-stands and 8 supported side leg lifts per side. Rest as needed."}, "mood_adjustment": "Tender or fearful of pulling pain: use small ranges and move slowly between positions. Restless: let the walk be rhythmic. Strong: add a second round, not deeper stretches.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Make iron easier to use: pair lentils, beans, greens, eggs or meat with a vitamin-C food such as citrus, guava, tomato or capsicum.', 'Drink regularly rather than waiting to feel very thirsty. Add lemon, mint or fruit if plain water is unappealing.', '{"heavy_day": "New, unfamiliar pulling pain this early can be unsettling — it''s a known, harmless pregnancy symptom, even when it doesn''t feel that way.", "a_little_low": "If a new ache worries you, it''s always fair to call your doctor to check rather than sit with the worry.", "okay": "Unclench your jaw, drop your shoulders and soften your hands. Let your body receive the message that it is safe to slow down.", "good": "Notice the moments today when your body feels genuinely comfortable.", "really_good": "Hold onto this — comfort is worth noticing, not just discomfort."}'::jsonb, 'Notice where clothing presses or rubs. A comfortable, supportive bra and softer waistbands can make the day noticeably easier.', 'Take a photo of something that made today feel calm. It does not need to be pregnancy-related.', 'Share one comfort change you need — slower plans, fewer late nights or more help with meals.', 'You listened before pushing. That is a form of strength.', 'Mention pain that is frequent, worsening, severe, or accompanied by bleeding, fever, urinary symptoms or dizziness.'),
  (16, 'second', 'Notice without comparing', 'There is no prize for feeling movement first.', 'Stay curious about your body without turning every sensation into a test.', 'Some parents begin to notice "quickening" around this stage; many do not until later, especially in a first pregnancy. Your baby''s movements are becoming more coordinated.', ARRAY['Flutters, bubbles or nothing recognisable yet','A more visible bump','Back or pelvic discomfort after long periods in one position','Changes in appetite, sleep or mood']::text[], '{"focus": "Build steady leg and upper-body support while balance is still comfortable.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 6 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 1 minute of slow marching while holding a stable surface.", "steady": "Complete two rounds: 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups, 8 supported heel raises and 30 seconds of easy marching.", "feeling_good": "Warm up with a 10-minute walk. Complete two rounds of 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 wall push-ups, 10 heel raises and 8 supported side leg lifts per side. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Low-energy: one unhurried round is enough. Distracted: count breaths instead of repetitions. Feeling good: use the full option while keeping every repetition controlled.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Choose a breakfast with protein and fibre: besan chilla with curd, eggs with whole-grain toast, or oats with milk/curd, nuts and fruit.', 'Fill one bottle at the start of your day and refill it as needed. Your urine should generally be pale yellow.', '{"heavy_day": "If you haven''t felt movement yet and are worried, that''s common — many first-time mothers feel it later. Mention it at your next visit if it''s on your mind.", "a_little_low": "Waiting for a milestone that hasn''t arrived yet is genuinely hard. You''re allowed to be impatient.", "okay": "When comparison starts, name five things you can see, four you can feel and three you can hear. Return to your own pregnancy.", "good": "If you''ve felt something, even something you''re not fully sure was baby, that flicker of recognition is worth savoring.", "really_good": "A first-movement day, whenever it comes, is worth marking."}'::jsonb, 'Use support where it helps: a cushion behind your lower back, comfortable shoes, and regular position changes.', 'Record a 30-second voice note: "This is what Week 16 feels like for me."', 'Tell your partner or support person that movement may be subtle and inconsistent at first — there is nothing to "perform."', 'Your pregnancy is unfolding on its own timeline, and you are meeting it there.', 'If you are worried about symptoms or movement, contact your care team; do not rely on a home Doppler for reassurance.'),
  (17, 'second', 'Support the body you are in', 'Comfort is a worthwhile goal.', 'Protect your back and pelvis during ordinary daily movement.', 'As your abdomen grows, posture and balance begin to shift. Your baby''s skeleton continues to harden and body fat begins to develop.', ARRAY['Lower-back tiredness','Round-ligament pulling with coughing, rolling or standing','Vivid dreams or interrupted sleep','More obvious breast or skin changes']::text[], '{"focus": "Practise movement patterns that protect your back during daily life.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Practise 6 slow hip-hinges with hands on your thighs, then walk for 2 minutes and stretch your calves.", "steady": "Walk for 6 minutes. Add two rounds of 8 hip-hinges, 8 wall push-ups and 8 bird-dog arm reaches at a wall or sturdy counter.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 12 minutes. Complete two rounds of 10 hip-hinges, 10 wall push-ups, 8 supported reverse steps per side and 8 standing opposite-arm reaches. Cool down gently."}, "mood_adjustment": "Sore: make this a mobility day and avoid loading painful movements. Mentally tired: use only familiar exercises. Energised: increase time, never speed or strain.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Create an easy snack pair: fruit with nuts, roasted chana with fruit, curd with seeds, or cheese/paneer with whole-grain crackers.', 'Have a drink before and after movement. In hot or humid weather, take extra care to cool down and replace fluids.', '{"heavy_day": "It''s fine to need more rest than you think you \"should.\"", "a_little_low": "Physical discomfort wearing on your mood is a real connection, not weakness.", "okay": "Give yourself a two-minute \"nothing to solve\" pause. Sit, breathe, and let unfinished tasks remain unfinished.", "good": "No need to justify feeling good today.", "really_good": "Let it be simple — a good day doesn''t need an explanation."}'::jsonb, 'If sleep is less comfortable, try side-lying with a pillow between your knees or under your abdomen. Choose what feels best; reposition whenever you need to.', 'Make a tiny "comfort menu" with three things that reliably help: music, fresh air, a warm shower, stretching or calling someone safe.', 'Ask for practical help with lifting, carrying or repetitive bending.', 'You made daily life kinder to your changing body.', 'Discuss persistent pelvic or back pain, pain that limits walking, numbness, weakness, or pain with urinary symptoms.'),
  (18, 'second', 'Prepare, then breathe', 'A scan is information, not a performance.', 'Go into the anatomy-scan window informed and emotionally supported.', 'A detailed ultrasound is commonly offered around 18-22 weeks to look at growth and anatomy. Sometimes every view cannot be obtained in one visit; that alone does not mean something is wrong.', ARRAY['Clearer flutters, or still no definite movement','A stretching or itchy abdomen','Occasional dizziness when standing quickly','Excitement, worry, or both about the scan']::text[], '{"focus": "Use familiar movement to settle scan-week tension.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk slowly for 3 minutes, then take 6 long, comfortable breaths with relaxed shoulders.", "steady": "Choose a familiar 10-minute walk and finish with 5 minutes of calf stretching, shoulder circles and gentle side-to-side weight shifts.", "feeling_good": "Walk, swim or use a stationary cycle for 20 minutes at conversational effort. Add 10 minutes of easy mobility: shoulder rolls, wall pelvic tilts and supported calf stretches."}, "mood_adjustment": "Anxious: choose repetition and familiarity rather than learning something new. Emotionally drained: five minutes counts. Relieved or upbeat: enjoy 30 minutes without turning it into a test.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Prep one low-effort meal for appointment day: vegetable khichdi with curd, a paneer wrap, or a lentil soup with toast.', 'Unless your clinic tells you differently for the scan, follow your usual hydration routine. Ask ahead if a full bladder is needed.', '{"heavy_day": "Scan-day nerves are real, even in a healthy, uneventful pregnancy.", "a_little_low": "Write down the question beneath the worry. A clear question is easier to carry into an appointment than a cloud of \"what ifs.\"", "okay": "A calm wait is its own kind of steady.", "good": "Let a good scan be simply good news, without needing to brace for something else.", "really_good": "Seeing your baby clearly on screen is a real, big moment — let it feel that way."}'::jsonb, 'Wear easy, comfortable clothing to the scan. Bring your records, medication list and any questions your clinic has asked you to prepare.', 'After the appointment, save one memory: a note, a scan image if provided, or simply how you felt walking out.', 'If you want company and your clinic permits it, invite your chosen support person. Tell them whether you want quiet reassurance or active note-taking.', 'You showed up for a meaningful part of your care, whatever emotions came with it.', 'Ask when and how results will be explained, what follow-up (if any) is recommended, and whom to contact with questions.'),
  (19, 'second', 'Trust the gradual changes', 'Subtle progress is still progress.', 'Build steady routines that support energy and digestion.', 'Your blood volume is expanding and your growing uterus can slow digestion. Your baby''s movements may become easier to recognise, though patterns are not established for everyone yet.', ARRAY['Constipation, bloating or heartburn','Leg cramps or restless sleep','Movement that is still sporadic','A stronger appetite or quicker fullness']::text[], '{"focus": "Break up stillness to support circulation, digestion and back comfort.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk around your space for 3 minutes, then do 10 heel raises and 10 ankle circles per side.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes. Add 8 chair sit-to-stands, 10 heel raises and a supported upper-back stretch.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 20 minutes, ideally after a meal if comfortable. Complete two rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 10 heel raises and 8 wall push-ups."}, "mood_adjustment": "Bloated or uncomfortable: choose gentle walking and stay upright. Flat mood: pair movement with one favourite song. Energetic: use the full duration but keep the talk test.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Support digestion with fibre plus fluid: oats, fruit, vegetables, whole grains, lentils and beans. Increase fibre gradually if your usual intake is low.', 'Spread drinks through the day. If heartburn is bothersome, try smaller amounts more often instead of large drinks with meals.', '{"heavy_day": "Digestive discomfort dragging your mood down is a real link, not you being dramatic about \"just\" constipation or heartburn.", "a_little_low": "It''s fair to feel frustrated by a body that keeps changing under you.", "okay": "Choose one expectation to lower today. \"Good enough\" can be the most caring standard.", "good": "Enjoy the easier stretches of the day.", "really_good": "Take it in fully — a genuinely good day counts for a lot right now."}'::jsonb, 'For an itchy stretching belly, use a plain moisturiser for comfort. Creams cannot reliably prevent stretch marks, and stretch marks are not a failure of care.', 'Build a three-song "reset playlist" for walks, chores or difficult afternoons.', 'Ask someone to share food prep: washing fruit, chopping vegetables or packing tomorrow''s snack.', 'A routine does not have to be perfect to support you.', 'Seek advice for severe or persistent abdominal pain, vomiting that prevents fluids, blood in stool, or constipation that does not improve.'),
  (20, 'second', 'Halfway, pause here', 'You are allowed to feel proud, uncertain, relieved or all three.', 'Mark the midpoint with care, not pressure.', 'Around this stage, the top of the uterus may be near the navel. Movement may feel clearer, while the anatomy-scan window continues through about Week 22.', ARRAY['More recognisable kicks or rolls, or movement that is still faint','A noticeably changing shape and posture','Breathlessness with effort','New feelings about visibility, identity or the months ahead']::text[], '{"focus": "Celebrate what your body can do at the midpoint, without comparison.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Take a 5-minute \"halfway walk\" and notice three things your body is doing for you today.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes, then complete 5 minutes of your favourite mobility moves from this trimester.", "feeling_good": "Walk, swim or cycle for 20 minutes. Add two gentle rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 8 supported side leg lifts per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Emotional: let movement be reflective, not productive. Tired: take the five-minute walk. Celebratory: choose the activity you genuinely enjoy rather than the one that looks most impressive.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Choose a celebratory meal that also feels good afterwards. A balanced thali, grilled fish or paneer with vegetables, or a favourite home meal all count.', 'Make hydration part of the celebration: a chilled water bottle, coconut water where food-safe and suitable, or infused water — without turning it into a target to "win."', '{"heavy_day": "A milestone week doesn''t erase however you actually feel today — let both be true.", "a_little_low": "Halfway can bring up \"how will the rest go\" worry. That''s normal, and you don''t need an answer today.", "okay": "Try a halfway check-in: What feels easier now? What is hard? What support would make the next month gentler?", "good": "Let yourself feel accomplished — you''ve genuinely done something hard.", "really_good": "This is worth marking, in whatever way feels like you."}'::jsonb, 'Take a bump photo only if it feels meaningful. You do not owe anyone documentation, sharing or a particular kind of joy.', 'Write or record three lines to your future self: what you have carried, what you have learned and what you want to protect.', 'Create a small midpoint ritual — a favourite meal, a quiet photo, a walk or ten minutes talking without logistics.', 'Halfway is not "only halfway." Look at how much your body and life have already held.', 'From 20 weeks onward, contact your clinician promptly for a severe headache that will not go away, vision changes, swelling of the face or hands, upper-abdominal pain, or difficulty breathing.'),
  (21, 'second', 'Make everyday life easier', 'Small adjustments count as real care.', 'Reduce friction in the routines you repeat every day.', 'Your centre of gravity continues to shift, and baby movements may become more noticeable. Skin stretching, mild swelling and back discomfort can also appear.', ARRAY['More consistent movement, but not necessarily a schedule','Backache after standing or sitting','Mild ankle or foot swelling later in the day','Skin colour changes or itchiness']::text[], '{"focus": "Support balance, calves and back as your centre of gravity shifts.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Hold a stable chair and complete 10 heel raises, 8 side-to-side weight shifts and a 1-minute easy walk.", "steady": "Walk for 7 minutes. Add two rounds of 10 heel raises, 8 supported side steps per direction and 8 wall push-ups.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 15 minutes. Complete two rounds of 10 heel raises, 10 supported side steps per direction, 10 wall push-ups and 8 chair sit-to-stands. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Unsteady: use support and skip balance challenges. Frustrated: focus on strong, slow repetitions. Feeling capable: extend the walk rather than removing support.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Make lunch easier to assemble: roti with dal and vegetables, curd rice with a protein side, or a whole-grain sandwich/wrap with paneer, egg, chicken or beans.', 'Keep water at the place where you spend the most time. Take it with you whenever you leave home.', '{"heavy_day": "If today felt like just getting through it, that still counts as a real day of care.", "a_little_low": "Small discomforts adding up is a legitimate reason to feel worn down, not something to push past silently.", "okay": "Ask: \"What is the next kind thing, not the whole plan?\" Do only that.", "good": "Notice what felt easier today, and why — worth remembering.", "really_good": "A genuinely good day deserves to be enjoyed without bracing for tomorrow."}'::jsonb, 'Choose supportive footwear and change positions often. Elevating your feet briefly may ease ordinary end-of-day swelling.', 'Wear, use or revisit something that feels distinctly like you — not just "pregnant you."', 'Ask your support person to notice one household routine they can fully own this week.', 'You improved a real day, not an imaginary perfect one.', 'Call promptly for sudden or one-sided swelling, calf pain/redness, swelling with headache or vision changes, or difficulty breathing.'),
  (22, 'second', 'Prepare without overbuying', 'You can research slowly and decide later.', 'Identify what may support your comfort before shopping for baby gear.', 'Your baby''s hearing is developing, and movement may be increasingly familiar. Your body may need more support during sleep, walking and work.', ARRAY['Stronger movement at certain times','Pelvic heaviness or lower-back fatigue','More discharge without irritation or strong odour','Changing sleep positions']::text[], '{"focus": "Build comfortable lower-body strength for walking, stairs and daily tasks.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 supported side steps per direction and 30 seconds of calf stretching per side.", "steady": "Complete two rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 10 supported side steps per direction, 10 heel raises and 8 wall push-ups.", "feeling_good": "Warm up with a 10-minute walk. Complete two rounds of 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 supported side steps per direction, 10 heel raises and 10 wall push-ups. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Heavy pelvis or back: shorten the range and stop any move that increases pressure or pain. Bored: change the music or location. Strong: complete the full routine with steady breathing.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Plan one "emergency meal" using safe staples: dal and frozen vegetables, eggs and toast, or a bean-and-rice bowl. Convenience is a care tool.', 'Use meals as anchors: drink with breakfast, lunch and dinner, then add regular sips between them.', '{"heavy_day": "Feeling behind on \"preparing\" is common and not a reflection of how ready you actually are.", "a_little_low": "It''s fine to feel overwhelmed by decisions you haven''t made yet — most of them can genuinely wait.", "okay": "Spend five minutes researching one comfort item — not an entire registry. Stop when you have enough information for today.", "good": "Enjoy the parts of preparing that feel fun, without turning the rest into pressure.", "really_good": "Let excitement about what''s coming be exactly that — excitement, nothing more required."}'::jsonb, 'If you need it, compare supportive bras, pillows or footwear. Fit and comfort matter more than pregnancy branding.', 'Read, sing or play one song aloud. Bonding can be ordinary and private.', 'Choose one baby-related decision to explore together and one you are deliberately postponing.', 'You prepared without letting preparation take over the day.', 'Report watery fluid that keeps leaking, bleeding, painful contractions, fever, or discharge with strong odour, itching or burning.'),
  (23, 'second', 'Protect your energy', 'Rest before you are emptied out.', 'Use your better-energy windows wisely and leave margin around them.', 'Your baby is adding weight and practising movements. You may feel more physically pregnant now, even on days when energy is good.', ARRAY['More obvious kicks, rolls or stretches','Breathlessness on stairs or hills','Leg cramps, backache or tired feet','A need for more recovery after busy days']::text[], '{"focus": "Protect energy by matching effort to how you feel before you begin.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk for 3 minutes, then take 2 minutes for shoulder rolls, ankle circles and slow breathing.", "steady": "Choose 10 minutes of walking, swimming or stationary cycling. Finish with 5 minutes of easy mobility.", "feeling_good": "Do 20 minutes of conversational aerobic movement. Add one easy strength round: 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 wall push-ups, 10 heel raises and 8 side leg lifts per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Depleted: stop at five minutes even if the plan says more. Stressed: choose rhythmic aerobic movement. Energised: use 30 minutes but rate the effort as moderate, not all-out.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Pair calcium-rich foods with your routine: milk, curd/yoghurt, paneer, calcium-set tofu or other fortified options that fit your diet.', 'Drink before, during and after activity. Dark urine, dizziness or a racing heart can signal that you need fluids and a pause.', '{"heavy_day": "Depleted is a real, physical state, not a personal failing — rest is the correct response, not a consolation prize.", "a_little_low": "It''s fair to resent needing more recovery time than you used to.", "okay": "Schedule ten minutes of recovery after the busiest part of your day. Treat it as part of the plan, not a reward.", "good": "Notice which parts of today gave you energy back — worth repeating.", "really_good": "A good-energy day is worth using well, and worth enjoying fully."}'::jsonb, 'A warm (not hot) shower, gentle foot care and loose clothing can help after a long day. Avoid heat that makes you dizzy or overheated.', 'Take yourself on a small date: tea, a balcony break, a bookstore visit or an unrushed snack.', 'Tell your support person what "protect my energy" looks like in practice this week.', 'You stopped before your body had to shout.', 'Contact your clinician for breathlessness at rest, chest pain, fainting, a racing heart that does not settle, or calf pain/swelling.'),
  (24, 'second', 'Screening is information', 'A test result is not a grade.', 'Prepare calmly for the usual gestational-diabetes screening window.', 'Glucose screening is commonly offered between 24 and 28 weeks, though timing and method vary. Gestational diabetes is influenced by pregnancy hormones; it is not a moral judgment about food or willpower.', ARRAY['More thirst or urination may occur in normal pregnancy and do not diagnose diabetes','More established movement for some parents','Rib, back or pelvic discomfort','Mixed feelings about tests and results']::text[], '{"focus": "Use regular movement for wellbeing, not to earn food or influence a test result.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Take a comfortable 5-minute walk, especially after a meal if that feels good.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes. Add 5 minutes of wall pelvic tilts, heel raises and a supported chest-opening stretch.", "feeling_good": "Walk, swim or cycle for 20 minutes. Add two rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 10 heel raises."}, "mood_adjustment": "Worried about screening: choose movement that calms you; do not exercise intensely to \"prepare\" for the test. Tired after an appointment: rest or choose five minutes. Well-rested: follow your normal moderate option.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Do not "eat perfectly" for the test. Follow your clinic''s preparation instructions. For everyday meals, combine carbohydrate foods with protein, fibre and fat for satisfaction.', 'Ask whether plain water is allowed during your test and follow the clinic''s instructions exactly. Bring water for afterwards if permitted.', '{"heavy_day": "Waiting on any test result can bring up worry, even a routine one done for nearly every pregnant mother.", "a_little_low": "It''s fair to feel nervous about a screening test — that doesn''t mean you expect a bad result, it just means it matters to you.", "okay": "Write down the practical questions: Do I need to fast? How long will I be there? When will results arrive? What happens after an abnormal screen?", "good": "Let good news be simply good, without needing to double-check it.", "really_good": "Relief is a real, valid feeling to sit in fully."}'::jsonb, 'Plan a comfortable appointment day: easy clothing, something to read, and a snack for afterwards if your clinic allows.', 'Save one non-medical moment from this week — a photo, a joke, a meal or a voice note.', 'Ask someone to handle transport, scheduling or a meal on screening day if that would reduce stress.', 'You took care of your health by getting information.', 'Follow up on results and next steps. An abnormal screening result may require another test; it does not by itself confirm gestational diabetes.'),
  (25, 'second', 'Learn your baby''s ordinary', 'Connection grows through attention, not perfection.', 'Notice movement naturally without forcing a formal count unless advised.', 'Movements may feel stronger and more distinctive, but every baby and placenta position affects how movement is felt. Formal movement-counting guidance often begins later and varies by care plan.', ARRAY['Kicks, rolls, stretches or hiccup-like rhythms','Movement that is easier to feel at rest','Pelvic pressure or backache','Sleep interrupted by comfort or activity']::text[], '{"focus": "Combine mobility and strength without using movement as a way to test the baby.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 shoulder rolls in each direction and a 2-minute walk.", "steady": "Walk for 7 minutes. Complete two rounds of 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 wall push-ups and 8 supported reverse steps per side.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 15 minutes. Complete two rounds of 10 wall pelvic tilts, 10 wall push-ups, 8 supported reverse steps per side and 10 heel raises. Cool down gently."}, "mood_adjustment": "Preoccupied with movement sensations: use the routine for your own wellbeing, not to provoke or check movement. Anxious about a change: contact your care team. Feeling good: enjoy the full routine at moderate effort.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Build a portable snack kit for busy days: nuts or roasted chana, fruit, whole-grain crackers, or a safely stored dairy/protein option.', 'Pair movement awareness with a normal pause and a drink — not as a trick to "make" baby move, but as a moment to settle and notice.', '{"heavy_day": "If sensations feel confusing or hard to place today, that''s a normal part of learning your baby''s patterns, not a sign you''re missing something.", "a_little_low": "It''s okay to feel a little disconnected some days even as movement becomes more familiar overall.", "okay": "Sit quietly for two minutes and notice what is present — movement, stillness, sounds, breath — without demanding a specific outcome.", "good": "Enjoy recognizing your baby''s rhythms today, without needing to analyze them.", "really_good": "You know your baby''s rhythms a little now. That''s a real kind of knowing, worth feeling good about."}'::jsonb, 'Protect sleep where you can: dim lights earlier, keep the room comfortable and use pillows to support your preferred side-lying position.', 'Create a short playlist for this stage of pregnancy. Include songs for you, not only songs "for baby."', 'Invite your partner to feel movement if and when you want to. If the timing does not work, let it be light rather than disappointing.', 'You are learning a new language of sensations one day at a time.', 'If movement has become usual for you and then clearly decreases or changes, contact your care team rather than waiting for the next appointment.'),
  (26, 'second', 'Second-trimester graduation', 'Look how much you have carried already.', 'Close this trimester with reflection, practical preparation and pride.', 'You are approaching the third trimester. Your uterus, blood volume and daily physical workload have all grown, and your baby continues to gain weight and mature.', ARRAY['Stronger movement and a more prominent bump','More back, rib or pelvic discomfort','Heartburn, breathlessness with effort or sleep changes','Excitement, impatience, worry, or emotional neutrality']::text[], '{"focus": "Finish the trimester with a routine you can adapt, not outgrow.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Choose your favourite two movements from this trimester and perform them gently for 5 minutes total.", "steady": "Walk for 7 minutes, then complete one round of 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 wall push-ups, 10 heel raises and 8 supported side steps per direction.", "feeling_good": "Warm up for 5 minutes. Complete 15 minutes of walking, swimming or stationary cycling, then one to two rounds of your favourite supported strength moves. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Uncomfortable: choose mobility and comfort. Proud and energised: repeat favourites without chasing personal records. Apprehensive about the third trimester: note which options reliably help; these become your adaptable baseline.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Make a third-trimester support plan: three easy meals, three snacks and one person or service you can call on when cooking feels hard.', 'Refresh your system for the next trimester: place bottles where you rest, work and leave home. Follow any personalised fluid advice from your clinician.', '{"heavy_day": "If second trimester was harder than its \"honeymoon phase\" reputation suggested, that''s a real, valid experience — the label doesn''t have to match your reality.", "a_little_low": "Moving into the final stretch can bring up new nerves. That''s normal, not a step backward.", "okay": "Complete this sentence: \"In the next trimester, I want more support with...\" Let the answer be practical and honest.", "good": "Let yourself feel proud of getting through this trimester, in whatever shape you did it.", "really_good": "Celebrate it, in whatever way feels like you."}'::jsonb, 'Do a comfort audit: shoes, bras, sleep support, work setup and daily lifting. Change one thing now instead of waiting until discomfort is severe.', 'Create a second-trimester time capsule: one photo, one voice note, one favourite memory and one thing you are proud of.', 'Have a gentle planning conversation: appointments, help at home, birth education and how each of you communicates when anxious. You do not need to solve everything tonight.', 'You have carried this pregnancy for roughly six months. Pause before moving on. This trimester deserves its own ending.', 'Confirm upcoming appointments, glucose-screening follow-up if relevant, local vaccine recommendations, and when your care team wants you to begin monitoring movement more formally.')
on conflict (week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
